.Dd November 26, 2026
.Dt NVIDIA-FIRMWARE 8
.Os
.Sh NAME
.Nm nvidia-firmware
.Nd fetch and place the GSP firmware the NVIDIA GPUs of the machine need
.Sh SYNOPSIS
.Nm
.Op Fl -version
.Nm
.Cm detect
.Op Fl n
.Op Fl -no-delete
.Op Fl -keep-versions
.Op Fl -expect-card
.Op Fl -driver-version Ar version
.Op Fl -from Ar directory
.Nm
.Cm fetch
.Op Fl n
.Op Fl -driver-version Ar version
.Op Fl -from Ar directory
.Nm
.Cm list
.Op Fl -cards
.Op Fl -supported
.Nm
.Cm status
.Nm
.Cm verify
.Nm
.Cm install
.Op Fl n
.Fl -force Ar image
.Op Fl -driver-version Ar version
.Op Fl -from Ar directory
.Nm
.Cm install
.Op Fl n
.Fl -all
.Op Fl -driver-version Ar version
.Op Fl -from Ar directory
.Nm
.Cm remove
.Op Fl n
.Ar image
.Nm
.Cm clean
.Op Fl n
.Sh DESCRIPTION
.Nm
fetches the NVIDIA GSP firmware that the graphics adapters of the machine
need and places it in
.Pa /boot/firmware/nvidia .
.Pp
The firmware is never shipped with the package.
The package holds the pin table and the table of the chip families.
The pin table is the file
.Pa /usr/local/share/nvidia-firmware/versions.list ;
it says which file belongs to which driver version and how big it is and
what its hash is.
The tool fetches the file the adapters need and checks its size and its
hash against the pin table before it puts it in place: a file that does
not match is never placed and nothing of it is left behind.
.Pp
The file is fetched from the release of the driver on
.Lk https://download.nvidia.com/XFree86/FreeBSD-x86_64 .
A machine that has no network takes the file from a directory of its own
with
.Fl -from ;
the directory holds the files themselves, either flat or in a directory
per driver version.
.Pp
The release is never unpacked as a whole: the names of its members are
read from it and only the member that is wanted is taken out of it.
The number of bytes that is written is bounded by the size the pin table
gives, so that an archive cannot fill the disk of the machine.
The programs of the base system are run with their own path: the
.Pa PATH
of whoever runs the tool is never searched.
.Pp
The options
.Fl -libxo Ar json
and
.Fl -json
write the output as JSON instead of text.
.Fl -json
is an alias for
.Fl -libxo Ar json .
Every JSON object of the program carries the version of the program and
the version of the schema of the output; the schema version is bumped
whenever a field of the output is added, removed or changed.
.Pp
The commands are:
.Bl -tag -width Ds
.It Cm detect
Find the NVIDIA graphics adapters of the machine and place into
.Pa /boot/firmware/nvidia
exactly the files those adapters need.
The version used is the one given with
.Fl -driver-version ,
else the version the
.Vt hw.nvidia.version_pin
sysctl names.
A version that the pin table does not name is an error and not a reason
to use another one: the driver would get the firmware of a version it
does not want.
When nothing is pinned the newest version of the pin table is used.
Only the files that
.Nm
placed itself are ever removed, and only when no adapter present needs
them and they are not forced.
A file in the directory that the inventory does not name belongs to
somebody else; it is reported as foreign and left alone.
.Pp
A machine that has no PCI bus, such as a jail or a build host, has no
adapter to be found.
The command says why, changes nothing and succeeds, so that the package
can be installed there.
The firmware is placed on the host with
.Cm detect
after the machine has booted.
.Pp
With
.Fl -no-delete
nothing is removed.
The same effect comes from
.Va nvidia_firmware_no_delete=YES
in
.Pa /etc/rc.conf
or
.Pa /etc/rc.conf.d/nvidia_firmware .
Those two files are read with the same simple
.Nm name Ns = Ns Ar value
lines
.Xr rc 8
uses; no other syntax is understood.
A file that cannot be read is an error and nothing is removed: the tool
cannot tell that it may take the files away, so it takes none of them
away.
.Pp
With
.Fl -keep-versions
the files of an older version stay in place.
Without it the files of the older version are removed once the files of
the version that is going to load are in place.
.Pp
With
.Fl -expect-card
the command fails when the machine has no NVIDIA graphics adapter.
Without it the command places nothing, removes the files that are not
needed and succeeds.
.It Cm fetch
Place the files the graphics adapters of the machine need, and nothing
else.
Nothing is ever removed by this command and a file that is in place is
never replaced, so the command may run while the nvidia module is
loaded: a file that is not there yet is not the firmware of the driver
that is running.
The rule of
.Xr devd 8
in
.Pa /usr/local/etc/devd/nvidia_firmware.conf
calls this command when a graphics adapter of NVIDIA is attached to the
machine.
.It Cm list
List every file of the pin table: the driver version it belongs to, its
size, its hash, the chip families it serves and the lines of products of
those families.
With
.Fl -supported
the list is the table of the firmware the package supports and nothing
else is told: the list works when the boot directory cannot be read.
Without it the list also says whether the file is placed in
.Pa /boot/firmware/nvidia
and for which adapter.
.Pp
With
.Fl -cards
only the graphics adapters of the machine are listed: the PCI selector,
the device id, the family of the chip and the files it needs for every
driver version of the pin table.
.It Cm status
Show what is in
.Pa /boot/firmware/nvidia :
the size and the version of every file, the adapter each file serves, the
files that are forced and whether the no-delete setting is on.
.It Cm verify
Check the files in
.Pa /boot/firmware/nvidia
against the pin table.
A file that was changed, a file that is missing and a file of ours whose
driver version is not in the pin table are errors.
A foreign file is reported but is not an error.
.It Cm install
Place a file that no adapter needs and mark it forced.
.Fl -force Ar image
places one file of the version, and
.Fl -all
places every file of the version.
A file of somebody else that has the name of an image is never
overwritten: the install refuses it before it fetches anything.
The dry run fetches the files and checks them in a directory of its own
and says what it would place; it fails in the same way as the run that
follows it.
The result tells the files that are really in the boot directory; a file
that was not placed is named as one that was not.
.It Cm remove Ar image
Remove a file from
.Pa /boot/firmware/nvidia
and drop its forced mark.
The file is gone at once; it is not left for the next
.Cm detect .
.Pp
The command refuses to run while the loaded driver uses the file: unload
the nvidia module first.
A file that the inventory of the directory does not name belongs to
somebody else and is never removed.
When a card of the machine needs the file, the command warns about it and
removes it anyway.
.It Cm clean
Remove every file
.Nm
placed in
.Pa /boot/firmware/nvidia .
The command refuses to run while the nvidia kernel module is loaded.
.El
.Pp
Every command that changes something accepts
.Fl n ,
which prints what the command would do and changes nothing.
.Pp
Every command that changes the boot directory takes the lock of the
program, the file
.Pa /var/run/nvidia-firmware.lock ,
and reads the state of the module under it.
The check and the change are one thing then, and a run that finds the
lock taken changes nothing and says so.
The lock is in a directory that only root may write in: the commands that
change the boot directory have to run as root or with
.Cm doas .
A run that cannot take the lock says so, and says which rights it needs.
.Pp
The commands that take files away or replace them refuse to run while the
nvidia kernel module is loaded, and refuse to run when they cannot tell
whether the module is loaded: the tool does not change the firmware of a
driver that is in use and does not guess.
The state of the module is read again right before the boot directory is
changed, so that a driver that came up in the meantime stops the run.
The lock of the program is not a lock of the kernel:
.Xr kldload 8
does not take it, so a module that is loaded between the last reading of
the state and the change itself is not seen by the tool.
The window is as small as the tool can make it; a machine that loads the
driver on its own has to keep it out of the way of the tool.
The module has to be unloaded before the package is removed or upgraded
as well: the firmware cannot be taken away while the driver uses it.
.Sh ENVIRONMENT
.Bl -tag -width Ds
.It Ev NVIDIA_FIRMWARE_MIRROR
The directory the firmware is taken from when there is no network.
It holds the files themselves, either flat or in a directory per driver
version, or the release of the driver.
The option
.Fl -from
says the same thing on the command line and wins over this variable.
.It Ev NVIDIA_FIRMWARE_TEST_ROOT
.It Ev NVIDIA_FIRMWARE_TEST_PCI
.It Ev NVIDIA_FIRMWARE_TEST_KLD
.It Ev NVIDIA_FIRMWARE_TEST_PIN
.It Ev NVIDIA_FIRMWARE_TEST_LOCK
.It Ev NVIDIA_FIRMWARE_TEST_FAMILIES_ALLOC_FAIL
These variables belong to the test suite of the program.
They are ignored unless the program was built for the test suite, and the
installed program ignores them in every case.
.It Ev NVIDIA_FIRMWARE_TEST_ROOT
The root of a fake system.
The data directory, the boot directory and the two files of
.Xr rc 8
are looked for below it.
.It Ev NVIDIA_FIRMWARE_TEST_PCI
A file of device ids, one per line, that takes the place of the scan of
the PCI bus.
A line may name the PCI selector of the card after the device id.
The word
.Ql none
takes the place of a machine that has no PCI bus at all.
.It Ev NVIDIA_FIRMWARE_TEST_KLD
The word
.Ql loaded
or
.Ql not
for the state of the nvidia kernel module.
.It Ev NVIDIA_FIRMWARE_TEST_PIN
The version the
.Vt hw.nvidia.version_pin
sysctl would name.
.It Ev NVIDIA_FIRMWARE_TEST_LOCK
The file that takes the place of the lock of the program.
.It Ev NVIDIA_FIRMWARE_TEST_FAMILIES_ALLOC_FAIL
The number of the call that cannot make the list of the families bigger.
.El
.Sh FILES
.Bl -tag -width ".Pa /boot/firmware/nvidia/SHA256SUMS" -compact
.It Pa /boot/firmware/nvidia
The directory the firmware is placed in.
.It Pa /var/run/nvidia-firmware.lock
The lock of the program.
Every command that changes the boot directory holds it while it works.
.It Pa /boot/firmware/nvidia/SHA256SUMS
The inventory of that directory.
Every line names one file:
.Pp
.Dl sha256  image  version  forced  size
.Pp
The hash is the hash of the pin table, the version is the driver version
it belongs to, the fourth field is
.Ql forced
for a file that
.Cm install
placed and a dash for any other file, and the last field is the size of
the file in bytes.
.It Pa /usr/local/share/nvidia-firmware
The directory the package installs its data files into.
.It Pa /usr/local/share/nvidia-firmware/versions.list
The pin table: the driver version of every file, its size and its hash.
.It Pa /usr/local/share/nvidia-firmware/nvidia-firmware.families
The table that says which chip family a PCI device id belongs to and which
files each driver version needs for that family.
The program holds the same table inside itself, so that it can name the
family of a card when the firmware is not placed.
.It Pa /usr/local/etc/devd/nvidia_firmware.conf
The rule of
.Xr devd 8
that places the firmware when a graphics adapter of NVIDIA is attached.
.It Pa /etc/rc.conf
.It Pa /etc/rc.conf.d/nvidia_firmware
The files that hold the
.Va nvidia_firmware_no_delete
setting.
.El
.Sh EXIT STATUS
.Bl -tag -width Ds
.It 0
The command did what it was asked to do.
.It 1
The command failed.
.It 2
The command was used wrongly.
.El
.Sh EXAMPLES
Place the firmware the adapters of the machine need:
.Pp
.Dl $ nvidia-firmware detect
.Pp
See what would be placed, without touching anything:
.Pp
.Dl $ nvidia-firmware detect -n
.Pp
Place the firmware of a version that is not the newest one:
.Pp
.Dl $ nvidia-firmware detect --driver-version 580.178.04
.Pp
Take the firmware from a directory of your own:
.Pp
.Dl $ nvidia-firmware fetch --from /var/tmp/firmware
.Pp
Write the list of the files the package supports as JSON:
.Pp
.Dl $ nvidia-firmware list --supported --libxo json
.Pp
Write the version of the program:
.Pp
.Dl $ nvidia-firmware --version
.Sh SEE ALSO
.Xr devd 8 ,
.Xr kldstat 8 ,
.Xr rc 8 ,
.Xr sysctl 8
